            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <p>Last Wednesday, Xiao Wang, the advertising lead for a utility app, suddenly found himself locked out of his Google Ads account that had been running for three months. There was nearly $20,000 still in the balance, and all running campaigns had been paused. This situation would panic anyone.</p>
<p>But Xiao Wang didn't panic. He followed a standardized appeal process and successfully recovered his account after 5 days. Let me break down his handling process for you, so you know exactly what to do when facing similar situations.</p>
<h2>Step 1: Find Out the Real Reason for the Suspension</h2>
<p>After account suspension, many people's first reaction is "false suspension" and then submit an appeal directly. But Google never suspends accounts randomly—understanding the reason is key to solving the problem.</p>
<p>When Xiao Wang logged into Google Ads, the system directly displayed a violation notice: the account was suspended for "circumventing systems" policy. Simply put, Google believed his ads were trying to deceive or bypass the review mechanism.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Judgment Tip</strong>: After receiving the suspension email, look for keywords after SUSPENDED REASON. Common reasons include: payment issues, intellectual property complaints, misrepresentation, circumvention, dangerous or offensive content, etc. Different reasons correspond to completely different appeal channels and materials.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Xiao Wang recalled that a week before the suspension, he had made significant changes to ad creatives—to improve click rates, he embedded landing page loading speed testing code into the tracking link. This operation was identified by the system as "attempting to manipulate ad auctions," a typical circumvention behavior.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Prepare Appeal Materials—These Items Are Essential</h2>
<p>Knowing the reason, Xiao Wang started preparing appeal materials. He organized what needed to be submitted into a checklist:</p>
<p><strong>Required Materials:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Problem Description Document</strong>: Describe the violation fact in concise language, acknowledge the mistake but don't be overly humble. For example, Xiao Wang wrote: "Our understanding of ad tracking mechanisms was not accurate enough. We used settings in third-party tool testing that could be misinterpreted. These have now been completely removed."</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Correction Proof</strong>: This is the core of the appeal. Specify what changes you made, attach screenshots or operation records. Xiao Wang's correction proof included: deletion records of tracking code, screenshots of landing page re-approval, and descriptions of adjusted ad structure.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Compliance Commitment Letter</strong>: State how you will avoid similar issues in the future. Can be written from three angles: policy learning, team training, and review processes.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Common Pitfalls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Don't repeatedly emphasize "false suspension" in appeal materials, as this makes reviewers think you haven't recognized the problem</li>
<li>Don't submit multiple appeal requests at once, as this will be flagged as spam</li>
<li>Keep attachments under 10MB, PDF or images recommended</li>
</ul>
<h2>Step 3: Find the Right Appeal Channel and Monitor Replies After Submission</h2>
<p>With materials ready, Xiao Wang didn't directly click "Request Review." Instead, he did two things first:</p>
<p><strong>1. Confirm the Correct Appeal Channel</strong></p>
<p>Google Ads has multiple appeal channels: account-level appeals are submitted through the backend, payment issues go to Billing Support, and if your account involves brand or trademark complaints, you need to use the Rights Holder Complaint Tool. Xiao Wang's "circumventing systems" violation was account-level, using the standard appeal channel.</p>
<p><strong>2. Conducted Internal Pre-Review Before Submission</strong></p>
<p>He sent the draft appeal materials to team members for review, ensuring no typos, clear logic, and specific actionable correction measures. This step helped him discover one ambiguous expression.</p>
<p>On the third day after submission, Xiao Wang received Google's reply: "We have completed the review and your account access has been restored." Both the balance and ad data in the account were intact.</p>
<h2>Final Thoughts</h2>
<p>There's no universal template for Google Ads account appeals, but there's a reusable logic: <strong>Diagnose First, Then Prepare, Then Submit</strong>. More important than last-minute scrambling is establishing ongoing policy learning and material review processes in your daily operations.</p>
<p>If you're currently handling an account issue, check whether your materials are complete against the checklist above. If you're unsure about the violation reason, feel free to leave a comment describing your situation—we can help you make a preliminary judgment.</p>
